import sys, json

sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding='utf-8')

def load_rt(fname, lang='en'):
    with open(f'data/guidelines/esc-hf-2026/{fname}', 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:
        data = json.load(f)
    num = data.get('number', '')
    title = data.get('title', {}).get(lang, '')
    out = [f"### {num} — {title}\n", "| Recommendations | Class | Level |" if lang=='en' else "| Рекомендации | Класс | Уровень |", "|---|---|---|"]
    for sec in data.get('sections', []):
        for r in sec.get('rows', []):
            txt = r.get('text', {}).get(lang, '').replace('\n', ' ')
            cls = r.get('class', '')
            lvl = r.get('level', '')
            out.append(f"| {txt} | **{cls}** | **{lvl}** |")
    return "\n".join(out)

def load_t20(lang='en'):
    with open('data/guidelines/esc-hf-2026/t20-red-flags-cardiac-amyloidosis.json', 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:
        data = json.load(f)
    title = data.get('title', {}).get(lang, '')
    out = [f"#### Table 20: {title}\n", 
           "| Red Flag | TTR | AL |" if lang=='en' else "| «Красный флаг» | TTR | AL |",
           "|---|---|---|"]
    for r in data.get('rows', []):
        if r.get('kind') == 'section':
            sec_text = r.get('cells', [])[0].get('text', {}).get(lang, '')
            out.append(f"| **{sec_text}** | | |")
        else:
            cells = [c.get('text', {}).get(lang, '').replace('\n', ' ') for c in r.get('cells', [])]
            if len(cells) == 3:
                out.append(f"| {cells[0]} | {cells[1]} | {cells[2]} |")
    return "\n".join(out)

lines = []

# Section 10: Non-cardiovascular comorbidities
lines.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
lines.append("")
lines.append('## 10. Non-Cardiovascular Comorbidities</summary><div class="details-content">')
lines.append("")
lines.append("Non-cardiovascular comorbidities are exceptionally prevalent in patients with heart failure across all LVEF phenotypes. They impair functional capacity, deteriorate health-related quality of life, accelerate disease progression, increase non-cardiovascular and all-cause mortality, and frequently confound the assessment of circulatory congestion.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 10.1 Obesity")
lines.append("Obesity contributes directly to systemic hypertension, dyslipidaemia, and type 2 diabetes mellitus, while exerting detrimental mechanical, metabolic, and pro-inflammatory effects on myocardial structure and microvascular endothelium. Obesity is particularly prevalent in HFpEF (accounting for 30%–40% of European cases and up to 60%–70% in North America). Circulating natriuretic peptide concentrations are paradoxically lower in obese patients due to augmented clearance receptor expression and adipose tissue degradation, necessitating lower diagnostic thresholds.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("In patients with symptomatic heart failure and obesity, structured lifestyle modification targeting weight reduction is foundational. Following the landmark STEP-HFpEF and STEP-HFpEF DM trials, the glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist (GLP-1 RA) **semaglutide** (or dual GIP/GLP-1 receptor agonist **tirzepatide**) substantially improves physical symptoms, Kansas City Cardiomyopathy Questionnaire (KCCQ) clinical summary scores, 6-minute walk distance, and body weight, while reducing HF hospitalizations.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt18-obesity-in-hf.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 10.2 Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus")
lines.append("Type 2 diabetes mellitus is present in 35%–45% of patients with chronic HF. SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin) are recommended for all patients with HF and T2DM, irrespective of LVEF, providing simultaneous cardioprotection, renoprotection, and glycaemic control. Metformin remains recommended as a safe foundational agent in patients with eGFR $\ge 30\\text{ mL/min/1.73 m}^2$. Thiazolidinediones (glitazones) and the DPP-4 inhibitor saxagliptin are contraindicated due to increased risk of fluid retention and HF hospitalization.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 10.3 Chronic Kidney Disease and Hyperkalaemia")
lines.append("Renal dysfunction and heart failure coexist bidirectionally as cardiorenal syndrome. Moderate-to-severe CKD (eGFR $< 60\\text{ mL/min/1.73 m}^2$) is found in $> 50\%$ of HF cohorts. Foundational neurohormonal blockers (ARNI/ACE-I, MRAs, SGLT2-Is) should not be withheld or discontinued for modest transient declines in eGFR ($< 30\\%$) upon initiation, provided volume status and serum potassium remain acceptable. To prevent hyperkalaemia and allow optimal titration of MRAs and ARNI, modern potassium binders (patiromer, sodium zirconium cyclosilicate) are recommended. In patients with T2DM and CKD, the non-steroidal MRA **finerenone** reduces incident HF hospitalizations and slows renal disease progression.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 10.4 Iron Deficiency and Anaemia")
lines.append("Iron deficiency (ID) occurs in up to 50% of patients with ambulatory HF and $> 70\\%$ with decompensated HF, independent of anaemia. It is biologically defined as **serum ferritin $< 100\\text{ \\mu g/L}$**, or **ferritin $100\\text{--}299\\text{ \\mu g/L}$ with transferrin saturation (TSAT) $< 20\\%$**. Iron deficiency impairs skeletal and cardiac mitochondrial energetics, reduces peak exercise capacity ($pVO_2$), and increases hospitalizations.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("Intravenous iron repletion with **ferric carboxymaltose (FCM)** or **ferric derisomaltose (FDI)** is recommended to alleviate symptoms, improve functional capacity, and reduce HF hospitalizations in symptomatic HFrEF/HFmrEF patients with confirmed iron deficiency. Oral iron administration is ineffective and not recommended.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt19-iron-deficiency-in-hf.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 10.5 Cancer and Cardio-Oncology")
lines.append("Cancer and heart failure share common epidemiological risk factors and biological pathways. Antineoplastic regimens—particularly anthracyclines (doxorubicin), HER2 inhibitors (trastuzumab), VEGF signaling inhibitors, proteasome inhibitors, and immune checkpoint inhibitors—can induce cancer therapy-related cardiac dysfunction (CTRCD). Multidisciplinary evaluation by a cardio-oncology team is recommended, including baseline risk assessment, serial echocardiography with global longitudinal strain (GLS), and biomarker monitoring (hs-cTn, NT-proBNP). Pre-emptive neurohormonal therapy with ACE-I/ARB and beta-blockers is indicated in high-risk patients.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 10.6 Lung Disease and Sleep-Disordered Breathing")
lines.append("Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and asthma co-occur in 20%–30% of HF patients. Cardioselective beta-1 blockers (bisoprolol, metoprolol succinate, nebivolol) are safe, well tolerated, and not contraindicated in COPD. Both obstructive sleep apnoea (OSA) and central sleep apnoea (CSA, including Cheyne-Stokes breathing) are highly prevalent in HFrEF. Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) is indicated for symptomatic OSA. Conversely, **adaptive servo-ventilation (ASV) is strictly contraindicated in patients with HFrEF and predominant central sleep apnoea**, following the SERVE-HF trial which demonstrated increased cardiovascular and all-cause mortality.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt20-lung-disease-sleep-apnoea.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 10.7 Anxiety, Depression, and Cognitive Impairment")
lines.append("Clinically significant depressive symptoms and anxiety affect up to 40% of HF patients, correlating with poor treatment adherence, impaired self-care, frequent emergency visits, and elevated mortality. Routine screening using validated questionnaires (PHQ-9, GAD-7) is recommended. Cognitive impairment (vascular, neurodegenerative, or secondary to cerebral hypoperfusion) mandates simplified medication regimens, blister packaging, and direct family caregiver involvement.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 10.8 Frailty")
lines.append("Frailty is a multidimensional clinical syndrome characterized by decreased physiological reserve and vulnerability to stressors, affecting 30%–50% of elderly HF patients. Identification using standardized tools (e.g., Clinical Frailty Scale, Fried phenotype) is recommended to tailor therapeutic goals, individualize invasive treatment decisions, and incorporate structured physical rehabilitation and nutritional support.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt21-anxiety-depression-frailty.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 10.9 Other Non-Cardiovascular Comorbidities")
lines.append("- **Gout and Hyperuricaemia:** Hyperuricaemia is frequently provoked by loop diuretics and impaired renal clearance. For acute gout flares, low-dose colchicine is preferred; **NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors are strictly contraindicated** as they induce sodium/water retention, blunting of diuretic response, acute kidney injury, and precipitated HF decompensation. Allopurinol or febuxostat is indicated for chronic urate-lowering.")
lines.append("- **Thyroid Dysfunction:** Both hyperthyroidism (precipitating high-output failure, tachyarrhythmias, and worsening ischaemia) and hypothyroidism (inducing pericardial effusion, bradycardia, and diastolic dysfunction) should be diagnosed and treated according to endocrine guidelines. Baseline and serial TSH monitoring is mandatory in patients receiving amiodarone.")
lines.append("- **Infections and Vaccinations:** Respiratory viral infections (influenza, SARS-CoV-2, RSV) and Streptococcus pneumoniae represent potent precipitants of acute decompensation. Routine annual influenza vaccination, pneumococcal vaccination, and COVID-19 boosters are recommended.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt22-other-non-cv-comorbidities.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("</div></details>")
lines.append("")

# Section 11: Multidisciplinary Team Management, Long-Term Follow-up, and Patient Care
lines.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
lines.append("")
lines.append('## 11. Multidisciplinary Team Management, Long-Term Follow-up, and Patient Care</summary><div class="details-content">')
lines.append("")
lines.append("Heart failure is a complex, multisystem, chronic illness requiring an organized, multidisciplinary care delivery infrastructure that transcends acute episodic hospital care. Integrated disease management programmes deliver structured patient education, symptom monitoring, rapid medical optimization, lifestyle coaching, and psychosocial support, dramatically lowering HF rehospitalizations and improving overall survival.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 11.1 Multidisciplinary Team Infrastructure")
lines.append("The heart failure care team is centered around the patient and their family caregivers, orchestrated by specialized healthcare professionals with defined core competencies:")
lines.append("")
lines.append("![Figure 21: Core members of the heart failure team with roles and tasks](images/fig21_core_members_hf_team.jpg)")
lines.append("")
lines.append("**Figure 21: Core members of the heart failure team with roles and tasks.** Interdisciplinary collaboration between heart failure cardiologists, specialized heart failure nurses, clinical pharmacists, dietitians, cardiac rehabilitation physiotherapists, primary care physicians, palliative care specialists, and social workers across the continuum of care.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt23-multidisciplinary-management-hf.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 11.2 Patient Education, Self-Care, and Lifestyle Counselling")
lines.append("Effective self-care reduces admissions and enhances treatment durability:")
lines.append("- **Symptom Monitoring & Action Plans:** Patients must be educated to recognize early signs of fluid retention (e.g., sudden weight gain $> 2\\text{ kg}$ over 3 days, increasing ankle oedema, worsening orthopnoea) and follow an individualized diuretic adjustment plan.")
lines.append("- **Dietary Sodium & Fluid Intake:** Extreme salt restriction is unnecessary and potentially harmful; moderate sodium intake ($< 2.3\\text{ g/day}$ of sodium or $< 5\\text{ g/day}$ of salt) is advised. Fluid restriction ($1.5\\text{--}2.0\\text{ L/day}$) is reserved for severe hyponatraemia ($[\\text{Na}^+] < 130\\text{ mmol/L}$) or refractory volume overload.")
lines.append("- **Alcohol and Smoking:** Complete smoking cessation and avoidance of illicit cardiotoxic drugs is mandatory. Alcohol should be avoided entirely in alcohol-induced cardiomyopathy, and restricted to $\le 3$ standard units weekly in other forms.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 11.3 Exercise Training and Supervised Cardiac Rehabilitation")
lines.append("Structured, supervised exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation is recommended for all stable patients with heart failure, irrespective of LVEF. Regular exercise improves skeletal muscle oxidative capacity, endothelial function, peak oxygen consumption ($pVO_2$), autonomic tone, and quality of life, while reducing all-cause and HF hospitalizations.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt24-exercise-training-cardiac-rehabilitation.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 11.4 Remote Patient Monitoring and Telemonitoring")
lines.append("Telemonitoring encompasses both non-invasive strategies (daily automated transmission of body weight, blood pressure, heart rate, and symptom checklists) and invasive haemodynamic monitoring:")
lines.append("- **Invasive Pulmonary Artery Pressure Monitoring (e.g., CardioMEMS):** Implantable wireless PA pressure sensors detect subclinical haemodynamic congestion days to weeks before overt clinical symptoms develop. Titration of diuretics and GDMT based on remote PA pressure data significantly reduces recurrent hospitalizations in high-risk ambulatory patients with persistent symptoms.")
lines.append("- **Non-Invasive Telemonitoring:** Structured phone support or digital health platforms with specialized nurse oversight should be considered to detect decompensation early and facilitate medication titration.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt25-telemonitoring.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 11.5 Long-Term Protocolized Follow-up and Palliative Care")
lines.append("Following hospital discharge or treatment initiation, a structured surveillance protocol is recommended:")
lines.append("")
lines.append("![Figure 22: Long-term follow-up of patients with stable heart failure](images/fig22_long_term_followup_stable_hf.jpg)")
lines.append("")
lines.append("**Figure 22: Long-term follow-up of patients with stable heart failure.** Flowchart detailing recommended outpatient review intervals (early visit within 7–14 days post-discharge, every 3–6 months for stable chronic HF), routine safety laboratory tests (electrolytes, eGFR, iron status), periodic echocardiographic restaging, and triggers for specialist referral.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("#### Advance Care Planning and Palliative Integration")
lines.append("Palliative care should not be delayed until end-of-life care in extremis; early integrated palliative support should be introduced alongside active disease-modifying therapies in patients with severe symptoms, frequent hospitalizations, or high frailty. Discussions regarding advance directives, preferred place of care, symptom relief for refractory dyspnoea/pain, and timely protocolized deactivation of ICD shock therapies should be proactively conducted.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt26-long-term-follow-up-palliative.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("</div></details>")
