# Section 12: Specific Conditions
lines.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
lines.append("")
lines.append('## 12. Specific Conditions</summary><div class="details-content">')
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 12.1 Heart Failure and Pregnancy")
lines.append("Pregnancy imposes profound haemodynamic demands, including a 40%–50% increase in blood volume, elevated cardiac output, and physiological tachycardia. Women with pre-existing heart failure (Stage B or C) face elevated risks of maternal cardiac decompensation and adverse fetal outcomes. Pre-conception counseling, risk stratification using the modified WHO (mWHO) classification, and dedicated multidisciplinary Pregnancy Heart Team management are mandatory.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("#### Pharmacotherapy During Pregnancy and Lactation")
lines.append("- **Strictly Contraindicated (Teratogenic / Fetotoxic):** ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNIs, MRAs, SGLT2 inhibitors, and ivabradine must be discontinued prior to conception or immediately upon pregnancy confirmation (**Class III, Level C**).")
lines.append("- **Safe and Recommended Agents:** Beta-1 selective blockers (metoprolol succinate; atenolol should be avoided due to fetal growth restriction), hydralazine, oral nitrates, and loop diuretics (at lowest effective doses to prevent placental hypoperfusion). Digoxin may be utilized for rate control.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt27-heart-failure-and-pregnancy.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 12.2 Cardiomyopathies and Myocarditis")
lines.append("- **Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HCM):** Diagnosis relies on unexplained LV wall thickness $\ge 15\\text{ mm}$ ($\ge 13\\text{ mm}$ with positive family history). In symptomatic obstructive HCM, beta-blockers or non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers are first-line. The cardiac myosin ATPase inhibitor **mavacamten** is recommended in patients with persistent symptoms and LV outflow tract obstruction despite first-line therapy. Surgical septal myectomy or alcohol septal ablation is indicated in drug-refractory severe obstruction.")
lines.append("- **Dilated and Arrhythmogenic Cardiomyopathies:** Comprehensive multi-gene panel testing and cascade family evaluation are indicated to identify pathogenic variants (e.g., *LMNA*, *FLNC*, *TTN*, *RBM20*, *DSP*) carrying high risks of sudden cardiac death or rapid progression.")
lines.append("- **Acute Myocarditis:** Diagnosis combines clinical presentation, high-sensitivity troponin, ECG, and contrast-enhanced CMR (applying updated Lake Louise Criteria). Endomyocardial biopsy is indicated in fulminant, refractory, or complicated presentations to identify giant-cell, necrotizing eosinophilic, or sarcoid myocarditis requiring immediate aggressive immunosuppressive therapy.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 12.3 Cardiac Amyloidosis")
lines.append("Cardiac amyloidosis is an increasingly recognized cause of heart failure, characterized by the extracellular deposition of insoluble amyloid fibrils derived from misfolded transthyretin (**ATTR-CA**, wild-type or hereditary) or immunoglobulin light chains (**AL-CA**). Prompt identification is critical, as disease-modifying therapies halt or reverse progression when initiated early.")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_t20('en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("![Figure 23: Diagnosis and treatment of cardiac amyloidosis](images/fig23_diagnosis_treatment_cardiac_amyloidosis.jpg)")
lines.append("")
lines.append("**Figure 23: Diagnosis and treatment of cardiac amyloidosis in patients with heart failure.** Diagnostic algorithm integrating serum and urine immunofixation electrophoresis, serum free light chains (sFLC) ratio, and 99mTc-DPD / PYP / HMDP bone scintigraphy (Perugini grading). Treatment pathways distinguish AL amyloidosis (rapid haematology referral for chemo-immunotherapy) from ATTR-CA (TTR stabilizer tafamidis or acoramidis, and TTR gene silencer vutrisiran).")
lines.append("")
lines.append(load_rt('rt28-cardiac-amyloidosis.json', 'en'))
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 12.4 Adult Congenital Heart Disease (ACHD)")
lines.append("Heart failure in adult congenital heart disease is the leading cause of late mortality. Management should be centralized at tertiary ACHD centers, tailoring neurohormonal therapy to unique anatomies (systemic right ventricle, single ventricle / Fontan palliation) and evaluating timely surgical or percutaneous revision of residual hemodynamic lesions.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("</div></details>")
lines.append("")

# Section 13: Key Messages
lines.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
lines.append("")
lines.append('## 13. Key Messages</summary><div class="details-content">')
lines.append("")
lines.append("The 2026 ESC Guidelines for the management of heart failure summarize the fundamental clinical imperatives into **10 Core Key Messages**:")
lines.append("")
lines.append("1. **Unified Stages of Heart Failure:** Heart failure is a continuous spectrum spanning Stage A (at risk), Stage B (pre-heart failure with structural/functional changes or elevated biomarkers), Stage C (symptomatic clinical HF), and Stage D (advanced, refractory HF). Proactive intervention at Stages A and B prevents disease onset.")
lines.append("2. **Simplified LVEF Phenotypes:** HFrEF is redefined as LVEF $\\le 50\\%$ (incorporating former HFmrEF), recognizing that neurohormonal blockade confers equivalent clinical benefits across this entire range. HFpEF is defined as symptomatic HF with LVEF $> 50\\%$ and objective evidence of diastolic dysfunction.")
lines.append("3. **Rapid Initiation of FMT Quad-Therapy:** Foundational Medical Therapy for HFrEF consists of four pillars: ARNI/ACE-I, beta-blocker, MRA, and SGLT2 inhibitor. All four drug classes should be initiated promptly—ideally during index hospitalization—and titrated rapidly to target doses within 4–6 weeks.")
lines.append("4. **Evidence-Based Therapy in HFpEF:** SGLT2 inhibitors and MRAs form the backbone of disease-modifying pharmacotherapy in HFpEF to reduce hospitalizations. The GLP-1 RA semaglutide (or tirzepatide) is indicated in obesity-related HFpEF to reduce symptoms and body weight.")
lines.append("5. **Structured In-Hospital Decongestion:** In decompensated heart failure, prompt intravenous loop diuretic administration guided by urine spot sodium ($> 50\\text{--}70\\text{ mmol/L}$ at 2 hours) and hourly urine output ensures complete decongestion. Inpatient optimization of FMT and early post-discharge visits (within 7–14 days) prevent readmission.")
lines.append("6. **Timely Advanced HF Triage:** Patients with recurring hospitalizations, worsening renal function, persistent intolerance to neurohormonal blockers, or escalating diuretic requirements must be identified early using the **I NEED HELP** mnemonic and referred promptly to an Advanced HF center for LVAD or heart transplantation evaluation.")
lines.append("7. **Concomitant Atrial Fibrillation Management:** Thromboembolic risk must be evaluated with the revised **CHA2DS2-VA score**, prescribing DOACs in preference to VKAs. Catheter ablation is recommended as a first-line rhythm strategy in selected HFrEF patients with tachycardiomyopathy or high arrhythmia burden.")
lines.append("8. **Systematic Comorbidity Screening:** Routine screening and targeted management of non-cardiovascular comorbidities—specifically iron deficiency (treated with IV ferric carboxymaltose or ferric derisomaltose), CKD, diabetes, and sleep-disordered breathing—are mandatory components of holistic HF care.")
lines.append("9. **Non-Invasive Diagnosis of Cardiac Amyloidosis:** Cardiac amyloidosis should be actively sought in patients with unexplained LV hypertrophy, HFpEF, or carpal tunnel syndrome, utilizing bone scintigraphy and light-chain screening without requiring invasive myocardial biopsy. Early initiation of TTR stabilizers (tafamidis, acoramidis) or silencers (vutrisiran) halts disease progression.")
lines.append("10. **Multidisciplinary Team Care and Rehabilitation:** Coordinated management by multidisciplinary heart failure teams, enrollment in exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation, individualized remote telemonitoring, and proactive advance care planning substantially improve clinical outcomes, functional status, and quality of life.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("</div></details>")
lines.append("")

# Section 14: Gaps in Evidence
lines.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
lines.append("")
lines.append('## 14. Gaps in Evidence</summary><div class="details-content">')
lines.append("")
lines.append("Despite monumental therapeutic progress, critical gaps in clinical evidence remain to be addressed by future clinical trials and registries:")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 14.1 Definitions, Epidemiology, and Prevention")
lines.append("- Precise biological consensus regarding normal physiological reference ranges of LVEF across age, sex, and ethnic cohorts.")
lines.append("- Prospective natural history, epidemiological trajectory, and long-term prognosis of patients with heart failure with improved ejection fraction (HFimpEF), including the safety of gradual medical de-escalation.")
lines.append("- Comparative long-term efficacy and safety of novel multi-incretin agonists, non-incretin weight-loss molecules, and bariatric/metabolic surgery in reducing incident HF.")
lines.append("- Definitive clinical trials assessing whether SGLT2 inhibitors and non-steroidal MRAs prevent incident HF in non-diabetic individuals with isolated hypertension, obesity, or non-albuminuric CKD.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 14.2 Diagnostic Phenotyping and Artificial Intelligence")
lines.append("- Deeper biological, proteomic, and metabolomic subphenotyping of HFpEF to identify targeted therapeutic responder subcohorts.")
lines.append("- Clinical validation of artificial intelligence algorithms (deep-learning ECG, automated echocardiographic analysis, generative clinical modeling) in early screening, risk stratification, and trial enrollment.")
lines.append("- Prospectively validated, non-invasive diagnostic criteria replacing invasive exercise right heart catheterization in ambiguous early-stage HFpEF.")
lines.append("- Clarification of BMI-adjusted natriuretic peptide decision cut-offs across morbidly obese and cachectic patient populations.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 14.3 Pharmacological and Device Therapies")
lines.append("- Head-to-head randomized trials comparing steroidal (spironolactone, eplerenone) versus non-steroidal MRAs (finerenone) in HFrEF and HFpEF.")
lines.append("- Optimal timing, sequencing, and combination strategies of foundational medical therapy (FMT) in hospitalized patients with acute decompensated heart failure.")
lines.append("- Definitive efficacy of primary prevention ICDs in non-ischaemic cardiomyopathy in the modern era of universal FMT and CRT.")
lines.append("- Role of conduction system pacing (His bundle and left bundle branch area pacing) compared to biventricular CRT in patients with heart failure and wide QRS complexes.")
lines.append("- Safety and long-term durability of transcatheter tricuspid valve interventions (TEER, replacement) in heart failure with severe functional tricuspid regurgitation.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("</div></details>")
lines.append("")

out_text = "\n".join(lines)
with open('scratch/esc_hf_block4_en.txt', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(out_text)

